{"name":"Balsa Wood","hex":"#9a7550","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"balsa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large tree, Ochroma pyramidale, native to tropical America, with wood that is very light in weight.","The wood of this tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/balsa"},{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ochroma","blocks":[{"text":"Ochroma pyramidale, commonly known as balsa, is a large, fast-growing tree native to the Americas. It is the sole member of the genus Ochroma, and is classified in the subfamily Bombacoideae of the mallow family Malvaceae. The tree is famous for its wide usage in woodworking, due to its softness and its high strength compared to its low density. The name balsa is the Spanish word for \"raft\" and the Portuguese word for ferry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Balso_%28Ochroma_pyramidale%29_%2814723405443%29.jpg/330px-Balso_%28Ochroma_pyramidale%29_%2814723405443%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochroma"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a structural tissue/material found as xylem in the stems and roots of trees and other woody plants. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/500px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/1280px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg/500px-Ochroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Balsa in Limbe Botanical Garden, Cameroon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOchroma_pyramidalis_MS_3425.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Balsa.jpg/330px-Balsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Balsa.jpg/330px-Balsa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three different sizes of balsa wood stock","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABalsa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg/120px-Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg/250px-Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg/120px-Ochroma_pyramidale1FrancesWHorne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org